-- 049: career roadmap for qualifying as a solicitor in England and Wales (training contract / SQE route).
-- Facts checked against sra.org.uk, sqe.sra.org.uk and gov.uk on 14 Nov 2026; every link was opened and loads.
-- Adds the roadmap and maps UK job titles (trainee solicitor, NQ solicitor, ...) to it. Safe to re-run.

values ('solicitor-england-wales', 'Solicitor (England and Wales)', '23-1011.00', 'deep', $rm$A solicitor advises clients, drafts documents, runs transactions and disputes, and is regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA). Since 2021 most people qualify through the Solicitors Qualifying Examination (SQE): a degree or equivalent (any subject), SQE1 and SQE2, two years of qualifying work experience (QWE) and a character and suitability check. A training contract is the most structured way to get that work experience: a firm employs you as a trainee for two years, usually across four seats, and often pays for your SQE preparation. Large firms recruit about two years before you start, mainly through vacation schemes, so timing matters. This roadmap covers the whole path: choosing a route, winning a training contract, passing the SQE, qualifying, and building a career after qualification. If you need a visa, check the Graduate visa rules early: it lasts 2 years if you apply on or before 31 December 2026 and 18 months from 1 January 2027.$rm$, $rm${"soc": "23-1011.00", "role": "Solicitor (England and Wales)", "depth": "deep", "occupation": "Lawyers", "summary": "A solicitor advises clients, drafts documents, runs transactions and disputes, and is regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA). Since 2021 most people qualify through the Solicitors Qualifying Examination (SQE): a degree or equivalent (any subject), SQE1 and SQE2, two years of qualifying work experience (QWE) and a character and suitability check. A training contract is the most structured way to get that work experience: a firm employs you as a trainee for two years, usually across four seats, and often pays for your SQE preparation. Large firms recruit about two years before you start, mainly through vacation schemes, so timing matters. This roadmap covers the whole path: choosing a route, winning a training contract, passing the SQE, qualifying, and building a career after qualification. If you need a visa, check the Graduate visa rules early: it lasts 2 years if you apply on or before 31 December 2026 and 18 months from 1 January 2027.", "stages": [{"level": "Foundations", "goal": "Understand how the solicitor profession works in England and Wales, pick your route, and test that you like the work before you spend money or years on it. Learn the legal system, the main types of firm, the SQE route and how training contracts are recruited, then get your first taste of real legal work through pro bono, open days and free virtual experience.", "weeks": 12, "plan": [{"weeks": "1-3", "focus": "Learn the structure: courts, sources of law, solicitors vs barristers, the regulator (SRA) and the professional body (Law Society). Read the SRA's SQE pages end to end."}, {"weeks": "4-6", "focus": "Choose your route (SQE with a training contract, solicitor apprenticeship, QWE built from paralegal roles, or the qualified-lawyer route) and check visa timing if you are not a UK national."}, {"weeks": "7-9", "focus": "Map the market: City, US firms in London, national and regional firms, high-street and legal aid firms, in-house teams. Shortlist 20 firms using Chambers Student and LawCareers.Net."}, {"weeks": "10-12", "focus": "Get first-hand exposure: two virtual job simulations, one open day or insight event, and sign up to volunteer at a legal advice clinic."}], "skills": [{"name": "The legal system of England and Wales", "why": "Every application, interview and SQE question assumes you know where law comes from and which court decides what. It is also tested directly in SQE1 (FLK1 'Legal System').", "lesson": "Law in England and Wales comes from two main sources: legislation (Acts of Parliament and the regulations made under them) and case law, where judges' decisions bind lower courts under the doctrine of precedent. Civil disputes usually start in the County Court or the High Court, which has three divisions (King's Bench, Chancery and Family). Appeals go to the Court of Appeal and then the Supreme Court. Criminal cases start in the magistrates' court; serious ones go to the Crown Court. Scotland and Northern Ireland have separate systems, so 'UK solicitor' usually means a solicitor of England and Wales. Example: a supplier sues a customer for £80,000 of unpaid invoices. That is a civil contract claim, probably in the High Court's Business and Property Courts or the County Court depending on value and complexity, decided by applying the Sale of Goods Act or contract case law to the facts.", "subtopics": ["Legislation, statutory instruments and how to read a section", "Precedent: binding vs persuasive, ratio vs obiter", "Civil courts: County Court, High Court divisions, Court of Appeal, Supreme Court", "Criminal courts: magistrates' and Crown Court", "Constitutional basics: parliamentary sovereignty, rule of law, judicial review", "Retained EU law, now called assimilated law"], "drill": ["Draw the civil and criminal court structures from memory on one page, then check them against the judiciary website.", "Pick one Act on legislation.gov.uk (for example the Consumer Rights Act 2015). Find the definitions section and restate one section in plain English.", "Read one recent Court of Appeal judgment on Find Case Law. Write three sentences: the facts, the question, and what the court decided and why."], "ready_when": "You can explain, without notes, which court would hear a given dispute, whether a past decision binds it, and where to find the up-to-date text of a statute.", "pitfalls": ["Mixing up the UK systems: Scots law is different.", "Reading an old version of a statute instead of the version currently in force.", "Treating every judgment as binding regardless of which court made it."], "check": [{"q": "A High Court judge decided a point last year. Is a Court of Appeal judge bound by it?", "a": "No. Higher courts are not bound by lower courts. The Court of Appeal is bound by the Supreme Court and, generally, by its own earlier decisions."}, {"q": "Where do you find the current, amended text of an Act?", "a": "legislation.gov.uk, checking the 'latest available (revised)' version and any outstanding changes."}], "resources": [{"title": "Courts and Tribunals Judiciary: how the courts work", "url": "https://www.judiciary.uk/", "provider": "Judiciary of England and Wales", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "legislation.gov.uk: UK statutes, as amended", "url": "https://www.legislation.gov.uk/", "provider": "The National Archives", "kind": "Free primary text"}, {"title": "Find Case Law: judgments of the courts of England and Wales", "url": "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/", "provider": "The National Archives", "kind": "Free primary text"}]}, {"name": "How you qualify: the SQE route and its alternatives", "why": "Choosing the wrong route, or misunderstanding the timing, can cost a year and thousands of pounds. Firms also expect you to understand the route they will put you on.", "lesson": "Since 1 September 2021 the main route is the Solicitors Qualifying Examination (SQE). To qualify you need: a degree or an equivalent qualification or experience (any subject), a pass in SQE1 (two exams, FLK1 and FLK2, testing legal knowledge with single-best-answer questions), a pass in SQE2 (practical legal skills), two years' full-time or equivalent qualifying work experience (QWE), and the SRA's character and suitability requirements. QWE can be gained in up to four organisations, paid or unpaid, and must be confirmed by a solicitor or a firm's Compliance Officer for Legal Practice (COLP). A 'training contract' is now simply the most structured way to get QWE: a firm hires you as a trainee for two years, usually rotates you through four six-month 'seats', and often pays for your SQE preparation. Other routes: a solicitor apprenticeship (earn while you study, no degree needed, SQE taken during it); building QWE from paralegal jobs and taking the SQE yourself; or, if you started a law degree, GDL or LPC before September 2021, the older LPC route, which stays open until the end of 2032. Lawyers already qualified abroad follow the qualified-lawyer route and may be exempt from parts of the SQE; QWE does not apply to them.", "subtopics": ["SQE1 and SQE2: what each tests", "QWE: four organisations, solicitor confirmation, retrospective claims", "Training contracts as structured QWE", "Solicitor apprenticeships", "The transitional LPC route", "Qualified lawyers and SQE exemptions", "Character and suitability"], "drill": ["Write your own route on one page: today's qualifications, which route you will take, each step, its cost and a target date.", "List every legal or legal-adjacent job you have done. Note which could count as QWE and which solicitor could confirm it.", "If you trained abroad, read the SRA qualified-lawyers page and note which SQE exemptions you could apply for."], "ready_when": "You can explain your exact route, its cost and its timeline in two minutes, and answer 'why not the other routes?'", "pitfalls": ["Assuming a training contract is legally required. It isn't, but many large firms still recruit this way.", "Paying for SQE preparation before checking whether a firm would fund it.", "Forgetting that QWE needs a solicitor or COLP to confirm it, so keep records as you go."], "check": [{"q": "How many organisations can your QWE be spread across?", "a": "Up to four, and it can be paid or volunteer work, as long as a solicitor or COLP confirms it."}, {"q": "Do lawyers qualified in another country need QWE?", "a": "No. QWE does not apply to qualified lawyers, and they may be exempt from parts of the SQE."}], "resources": [{"title": "Becoming a solicitor through the SQE", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/sqe/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Qualifying work experience (QWE) rules", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/sqe/qualifying-work-experience-candidates/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Solicitor apprenticeships", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/sqe/solicitor-apprenticeships/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Legal Practice Course (transitional) route", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/legal-practice-course-route/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Qualified lawyers: becoming a solicitor of England and Wales", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/qualified-lawyers/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "SQE exemptions for qualified lawyers", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/qualified-lawyers/sqe-exemptions/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Character and suitability", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/character-and-suitability/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Graduate visa", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/graduate-visa", "provider": "GOV.UK", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Skilled Worker visa", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa", "provider": "GOV.UK", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Check if you need a UK visa", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/check-uk-visa", "provider": "GOV.UK", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Becoming a solicitor", "url": "https://www.lawsociety.org.uk/career-advice/becoming-a-solicitor", "provider": "The Law Society", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "CILEX: chartered legal executive route", "url": "https://www.cilex.org.uk/", "provider": "CILEX", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Solicitor apprenticeship standard (level 7)", "url": "https://skillsengland.education.gov.uk/apprenticeship-standards/st0246-v1-1", "provider": "Skills England", "kind": "official"}]}, {"name": "Reading the legal market and choosing firms", "why": "Firms reject generic applications. 'Why this firm?' is the question that sinks most forms, and you can only answer it if you understand how firms differ.", "lesson": "Firms differ by clients, work and training. Magic Circle and other large City firms do big corporate, finance and disputes work for multinationals and banks. US firms in London have smaller trainee intakes, more responsibility and often higher pay. National and regional firms do a broader mix for mid-sized businesses. High-street and legal aid firms act for individuals in crime, family, housing and immigration. In-house teams and the Government Legal Profession also take trainees. For each firm, learn: its main practice areas and clients, intake size, number of seats and whether you can go on secondment or abroad, its trainee retention rate, recent deals or cases, whether it funds the SQE and maintenance, and whether it sponsors visas. Example of a strong 'why us': 'Your energy and infrastructure group advised on the financing of [a named project], and your four-seat structure guarantees a seat in that team. My placement in a renewables developer's legal team showed me that's the work I want.'", "subtopics": ["Firm types: Magic Circle, Silver Circle, US firms, national, regional, high street, legal aid, in-house", "Practice areas: corporate, finance, disputes, real estate, private client, employment, crime, family", "Trainee intake, seats, secondments and retention", "Funding: SQE fees, maintenance grants, visa sponsorship", "Following deals and the business news"], "drill": ["Build a spreadsheet of 20 firms: type, practice strengths, intake, seats, deadlines, funding, visa sponsorship, retention.", "For your top five firms, find one recent deal or case each and write two sentences on why it mattered commercially.", "Read one business news story a day and write one line on which practice areas it would create work for."], "ready_when": "For any firm on your list you can say what it does, who its clients are, one recent matter, and a specific reason it fits you, without looking anything up.", "pitfalls": ["Applying to 40 firms with the same answers.", "Quoting the firm's own slogans back to it.", "Ignoring whether the firm sponsors visas when you need one."], "check": [{"q": "What is a trainee retention rate and why does it matter?", "a": "The share of qualifying trainees a firm keeps on as newly qualified solicitors. A low rate means you may need to find an NQ job elsewhere."}], "resources": [{"title": "Chambers Student: firm guides and true picture reports", "url": "https://chambers.com/student", "provider": "Chambers Student", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "The Firms Most List (trainee and NQ data by firm)", "url": "https://www.legalcheek.com/the-firms-most-list/", "provider": "Legal Cheek", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Four areas of knowledge you need to get a training contract", "url": "https://www.lawcareers.net/Explore/Features/26102026-Here-are-four-areas-of-knowledge-you-need-to-get-a-training-contract", "provider": "LawCareers.Net", "kind": "reference"}]}, {"name": "Early exposure: open days, insight schemes and virtual experience", "why": "Firms want evidence that you have tested your interest in law. Early exposure also gives you real material for application answers and interviews.", "lesson": "Most large firms run open days, first-year insight schemes and diversity programmes, often a year or more before training contract recruitment. They are short and easy to apply for, they get you in front of graduate recruiters, and some lead straight to fast-tracked vacation scheme interviews. Free online job simulations let you do a realistic task (a due diligence review, a client email) in a few hours and talk about it credibly. Pro bono and advice-clinic volunteering gives you real client contact and can count towards QWE if a solicitor confirms it.", "subtopics": ["First-year insight schemes and open days", "Diversity and access programmes", "Virtual job simulations", "Pro bono and legal advice clinics", "Networking with trainees and recruiters"], "drill": ["Apply to three open days or insight schemes from this year's list.", "Complete two law job simulations and write a five-line summary of each task: what you did, what you found hard, what you learned.", "Contact one advice clinic or Citizens Advice office about volunteering."], "ready_when": "You have at least two concrete experiences you can describe as a situation, your action and the result, and you know what trainees at your target firms actually do day to day.", "pitfalls": ["Listing events attended without saying what you learned.", "Waiting for final year: many schemes are for first and second-year students."], "check": [{"q": "Why can volunteering at an advice clinic matter for qualification, not just applications?", "a": "If a solicitor or COLP confirms it, it can count towards your two years of QWE."}], "resources": [{"title": "Law firm open days and insight schemes 2026/27", "url": "https://www.lawcareers.net/Explore/Features/15092026-Law-firm-open-days-and-insight-schemes-202627", "provider": "LawCareers.Net", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Free law job simulations", "url": "https://www.theforage.com/simulations?careers=law", "provider": "Forage", "kind": "practice site"}, {"title": "LawWorks: pro bono legal advice clinics", "url": "https://www.lawworks.org.uk/", "provider": "LawWorks", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Volunteer with Citizens Advice", "url": "https://www.citizensadvice.org.uk/about-us/browse/volunteer-with-us/", "provider": "Citizens Advice", "kind": "reference"}]}], "project": {"title": "Personal qualification plan and firm shortlist", "brief": "Produce the plan you will run for the next two years: your route, your timeline, your budget and a researched shortlist of firms.", "steps": ["Write a one-page route plan: qualifications you hold, the route you will take, each step with its cost (SQE fees from the SRA's cost page) and target dates.", "If you need a visa, add the visa steps and deadlines (for example the date your Graduate visa would end) and mark which firms sponsor.", "Build a 20-firm spreadsheet with type, practice strengths, intake, seats, funding, sponsorship, retention and every deadline.", "Write a 150-word 'why this firm' paragraph for each of your top five, each citing a specific matter or feature.", "Book three events (open days, insight schemes or clinic volunteering) into your calendar."], "done_when": "Your plan has dates and costs for every step, your spreadsheet has all 20 firms filled in, and someone who works in law agrees your top-five paragraphs are specific to each firm."}, "evidence": ["SRA: to qualify through the SQE you need a degree or equivalent, SQE1, SQE2, two years' QWE and character and suitability (sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/sqe).", "SRA: QWE can be in up to four organisations, paid or volunteer, including placements and paralegal work, and must be confirmed by a solicitor or COLP; it can be claimed retrospectively.", "SRA: QWE does not apply to qualified lawyers, who may be exempt from parts of the SQE."]}, {"level": "Job-ready", "goal": "Win a training contract (or a paralegal role that gives you QWE). Write applications that get through the first screen, pass the online tests, do well on a vacation scheme, and convert it into an offer. Large firms usually recruit about two years before the start date, so this stage runs on their calendar, not yours.", "weeks": 40, "plan": [{"weeks": "1-4", "focus": "Build a deadline calendar from the listing sites and confirm every date on each firm's own careers page. Draft your application answer bank."}, {"weeks": "5-12", "focus": "Vacation scheme season: apply early (many firms review on a rolling basis), practise the Watson Glaser and situational judgement tests, and prepare for video interviews."}, {"weeks": "13-24", "focus": "Assessment centres: case studies, written exercises, group tasks and partner interviews. Keep applying for direct training contracts and paralegal roles in parallel."}, {"weeks": "25-36", "focus": "Vacation schemes: treat each as a two to four-week interview. Do the work well, ask good questions, and prepare for the end-of-scheme training contract interview."}, {"weeks": "37-40", "focus": "Decide on offers, check funding and visa sponsorship terms, and start any pre-joining SQE preparation the firm arranges."}], "skills": [{"name": "Writing application forms that get through", "why": "Most candidates are rejected at the form. Recruiters read hundreds, so the ones that win are specific, evidenced and free of errors.", "lesson": "Firm forms usually ask: why law, why this firm, why commercial law, a time you showed a particular competency (teamwork, resilience, attention to detail), and a commercial awareness question about a recent business story. Answer each with evidence, not adjectives. Use a simple structure: situation, what you did, the result, and what it shows about you as a future trainee. Keep a bank of 10 to 12 strong stories and adapt them; never paste the same 'why us' twice. Proofread out loud: one typo can end an application at a firm that tests attention to detail. Example: instead of 'I am a strong team player', write 'As treasurer of the debating society I rebuilt our budget after a 30% funding cut, by negotiating venue costs and finding two sponsors, so we ran the full competition season.'", "subtopics": ["Why law, why commercial law, why this firm", "Competency answers with situation, action and result", "Commercial awareness answers", "Work experience section that shows skills, not duties", "Word limits, tone and proofreading"], "drill": ["Write your answer bank: 10 stories, each in 150 words, tagged by the competencies it shows.", "Rewrite your work experience section so every line names a skill and a result.", "Swap one form with a peer or careers adviser and fix every vague sentence they mark."], "ready_when": "Each answer on your form contains a specific example and a result, your 'why us' could not be sent to any other firm, and there are no errors.", "pitfalls": ["Writing about the firm's 'prestige' or 'excellent training' without specifics.", "Describing duties ('I filed documents') instead of skills and outcomes.", "Submitting on the deadline day when the firm recruits on a rolling basis."], "check": [{"q": "What makes a 'why this firm' answer strong?", "a": "It links a specific feature of the firm (a practice area, a matter, the seat structure, a client sector) to specific evidence from your own experience."}], "resources": [{"title": "Writing the work experience section of an application form", "url": "https://www.allaboutlaw.co.uk/law-careers/law-firm-applications/online-application-forms-work-experience-", "provider": "AllAboutLaw", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "What a training contract or QWE is, and how to get one", "url": "https://targetjobs.co.uk/careers-advice/law/what-and-how-do-i-get-training-contract-or-qualifying-work-experience", "provider": "targetjobs", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Training contract guide", "url": "https://www.lawcareers.net/Solicitors/TrainingContract", "provider": "LawCareers.Net", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Training contracts: a plain overview", "url": "https://www.prospects.ac.uk/jobs-and-work-experience/job-sectors/law-sector/training-contracts/", "provider": "Prospects", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "The training contract and qualifying work experience", "url": "https://www.legalcheek.com/paths-to-becoming-a-lawyer/the-training-contract-qualifying-work-experience/", "provider": "Legal Cheek", "kind": "reference"}]}, {"name": "Online tests: Watson Glaser and situational judgement", "why": "Many firms screen with a critical thinking test before a person reads your form, and the pass mark is high.", "lesson": "The Watson Glaser test, widely used by law firms, has five sections: inferences, recognising assumptions, deduction, interpretation and evaluating arguments. The trap is using outside knowledge: you must judge only from the passage given. Situational judgement tests show you workplace scenarios (a missed deadline, a confidential document sent to the wrong person) and ask you to rank responses. The best answers usually protect the client and the firm, escalate honestly and early, and follow professional rules. Practise under timed conditions, then review every mistake and write down which rule of the test you broke.", "subtopics": ["Inference, assumption, deduction, interpretation, evaluation", "Answering only from the passage", "Situational judgement: integrity, escalation, client care", "Timing and review"], "drill": ["Do one timed practice test a week for six weeks and log your score by section.", "For every wrong answer, write one line explaining the rule you missed.", "Rank the responses to five workplace dilemmas, then compare with the SRA Principles."], "ready_when": "You consistently score well above your target in timed practice and can explain every answer you got wrong.", "pitfalls": ["Bringing in real-world knowledge the passage doesn't state.", "Taking the real test untried at an important firm."], "check": [{"q": "In a deduction question, a conclusion seems true in real life but doesn't follow from the statements. What do you answer?", "a": "That it does not follow. You judge only from the statements given."}], "resources": [{"title": "Watson Glaser critical thinking test: practice and format", "url": "https://www.watsonglaser.co.uk/", "provider": "Watson Glaser practice", "kind": "practice site"}, {"title": "Watson Glaser III (the publisher's description)", "url": "https://www.talentlens.com/watson-glaser.html", "provider": "TalentLens (Pearson)", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "SRA Principles", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/standards-regulations/principles/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}]}, {"name": "Commercial awareness", "why": "Commercial firms sell advice to businesses. They test whether you understand what clients care about: money, risk, deals and regulation.", "lesson": "Commercial awareness means understanding how businesses make money, what pressures they face, and how that creates legal work. Start from the client: a retailer facing rising costs may restructure leases (real estate), cut staff (employment), refinance debt (banking) or sell a division (corporate). For any news story, ask: who are the parties, what do they want, what are the risks, and which lawyers get instructed? Know the basics: how a company is financed (equity and debt), what an M&A deal is, why interest rates matter to deal volumes, and how regulation (competition, financial services, data protection) creates work.", "subtopics": ["How companies make money and raise finance", "M&A, private equity and capital markets basics", "Disputes and restructuring when the economy turns", "Regulation as a source of legal work", "Following deals in the legal press"], "drill": ["Each week pick one business story and write a 200-word note: parties, commercial aim, risks, and the practice areas instructed.", "Follow one deal from announcement to completion in the legal press and note which firms advised each side.", "Explain a recent deal to a friend in two minutes without jargon."], "ready_when": "You can discuss two current business stories in an interview, including what lawyers on each side would do.", "pitfalls": ["Memorising headlines without the 'so what' for clients.", "Only following the firm's own press releases."], "check": [{"q": "Interest rates rise sharply. Name two areas of legal work that might grow and one that might slow.", "a": "Restructuring and insolvency, and disputes, often grow; leveraged buyouts and other debt-funded M&A often slow."}], "resources": [{"title": "The Lawyer: UK legal market news", "url": "https://www.thelawyer.com/", "provider": "The Lawyer", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "BBC News: Business", "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/business", "provider": "BBC", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Four areas of knowledge you need to get a training contract", "url": "https://www.lawcareers.net/Explore/Features/26102026-Here-are-four-areas-of-knowledge-you-need-to-get-a-training-contract", "provider": "LawCareers.Net", "kind": "reference"}]}, {"name": "Assessment centres and vacation schemes", "why": "Many large firms recruit most of their trainees from their vacation schemes, so the scheme itself is the real interview.", "lesson": "Assessment centres usually include a written exercise (summarising documents into a client email), a case study with a presentation, a group exercise and a partner interview. Recruiters look for clear thinking, sensible prioritisation, client focus and how you treat others. On a vacation scheme you sit in a team, do real tasks such as research notes and document reviews, and attend talks and socials. Do every task carefully, confirm deadlines and format, ask for feedback, and note what you learn. The scheme usually ends with a training contract interview, where you'll be asked about your work and why you want this firm.", "subtopics": ["Written and case study exercises", "Group exercises: contributing without dominating", "Partner interviews and 'tell me about a deal'", "Vacation scheme tasks and feedback", "End-of-scheme interview"], "drill": ["Do a timed written exercise: summarise a 10-page set of facts into a one-page client email in 45 minutes.", "Run a 10-minute case study presentation for a friend and take two hard questions.", "Write down three questions you'd ask a partner that show you've researched the firm's work."], "ready_when": "You can produce a clear one-page client email under time pressure and present a recommendation with its risks in 10 minutes.", "pitfalls": ["Talking over others in group tasks.", "Treating vacation scheme socials as off the record.", "Not asking for feedback on your work during the scheme."], "check": [{"q": "In a group exercise someone keeps getting talked over. What should you do?", "a": "Bring them in by name and build on their point. Recruiters score how you work with others, not just what you say."}], "resources": [{"title": "Law training contracts: live listings", "url": "https://targetjobs.co.uk/law-training-contracts", "provider": "targetjobs", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Training contract deadlines", "url": "https://chambers.com/student/apply/training-contract-deadlines", "provider": "Chambers Student", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Key deadlines calendar (training contracts, vacation schemes, open days)", "url": "https://www.legalcheek.com/key-deadlines-calendar/", "provider": "Legal Cheek", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Training contract deadlines", "url": "https://www.legal500.com/future-lawyers/career-clinic/deadlines/training-contract-deadlines/", "provider": "Legal 500 Future Lawyers", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Training contract deadlines", "url": "https://www.thelawyerportal.com/deadlines/training-contract-deadlines/", "provider": "The Lawyer Portal", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Training contracts: listings and firm profiles", "url": "https://www.allaboutlaw.co.uk/law-jobs/training-contracts", "provider": "AllAboutLaw", "kind": "reference"}]}], "project": {"title": "Application season pack", "brief": "Run a full application season as a project: a calendar, an answer bank, test practice and interview preparation, with results tracked.", "steps": ["Build a deadline calendar for your shortlist from Chambers Student and Legal Cheek, then confirm each date on the firm's own careers page.", "Write your answer bank (10 stories) and a tailored 'why us' for each target firm.", "Complete six timed critical-thinking practice tests and log the scores.", "Write four commercial awareness notes on live stories and practise explaining each in two minutes.", "Submit at least 10 strong vacation scheme or training contract applications, early in each window.", "After each rejection or interview, record what happened and change one thing in your approach."], "done_when": "Every target deadline is met, every application has been reviewed by someone else, and you have a log showing your test scores and interview feedback improving."}, "evidence": ["Listing sites (Chambers Student, Legal Cheek, LawCareers.Net, targetjobs) publish firm-by-firm training contract and vacation scheme deadlines; dates should be confirmed on each firm's own site because listings can lag.", "SRA: a training contract is one way of completing qualifying work experience; QWE can also be gained in other legal roles."]}, {"level": "Advanced", "goal": "Pass SQE1 and SQE2, complete two years of qualifying work experience as a trainee, and be admitted as a solicitor. Learn the legal knowledge the SQE tests, then the practical skills: interviewing, advocacy, research, writing, drafting and case analysis, with ethics running through all of it.", "weeks": 104, "plan": [{"weeks": "1-24", "focus": "SQE1 preparation (often firm-funded): FLK1 (business, dispute resolution, contract, tort, legal system, legal services) and FLK2 (property, wills, trusts, land, solicitors accounts, criminal law and practice). Practise single-best-answer questions daily."}, {"weeks": "25-26", "focus": "Sit FLK1 and FLK2 in the same assessment window."}, {"weeks": "27-52", "focus": "First two seats of your training contract. Keep a QWE log of tasks and competences. Prepare for SQE2 using real work examples."}, {"weeks": "53-78", "focus": "Third seat; sit SQE2 (four oral and twelve written assessments). Start thinking about which team you want to qualify into."}, {"weeks": "79-104", "focus": "Final seat; apply for NQ roles at your firm; get QWE confirmed by a solicitor or COLP, complete character and suitability, and apply for admission to the roll."}], "skills": [{"name": "SQE1: functioning legal knowledge", "why": "SQE1 is a closed-book test of whether you can apply core law like a newly qualified solicitor. It covers far more ground than most degrees and many candidates fail it.", "lesson": "SQE1 has two exams, FLK1 and FLK2. Each is a set of single-best-answer questions: a short client scenario, then five options, only one of which is best. FLK1 covers Business Law and Practice, Dispute Resolution, Contract, Tort, the Legal System (including constitutional, administrative and EU law) and Legal Services. FLK2 covers Property Law and Practice, Wills and the Administration of Estates, Solicitors Accounts, Land Law, Trusts, Criminal Liability and Criminal Law and Practice. Ethics and professional conduct is tested throughout both. Tax is tested only within business, property and wills, and rates are given to you. You must book FLK1 and FLK2 in the same window. From September 2026 SQE1 costs £2,006. The law tested is the law as it stood four months before the window opens. Study each topic, then do many timed questions and review why each wrong option is wrong.", "subtopics": ["FLK1: business, dispute resolution, contract, tort, legal system, legal services", "FLK2: property, wills and estates, solicitors accounts, land, trusts, criminal liability and practice", "Ethics tested throughout", "Single-best-answer technique", "Timed practice and error logs"], "drill": ["Read the SQE1 Assessment Specification and turn it into a checklist of topics.", "Do 40 timed single-best-answer questions a day and keep an error log by topic.", "Each week, teach one topic to someone else in 10 minutes without notes."], "ready_when": "You score consistently above your target on full-length mixed practice papers in both FLK1 and FLK2, and your error log shows no topic you keep getting wrong.", "pitfalls": ["Only reading notes and not doing timed questions.", "Ignoring Solicitors Accounts and ethics because they feel small.", "Studying a topic once and never revisiting it."], "check": [{"q": "Can you take FLK1 in one window and FLK2 in the next?", "a": "Normally no. Both must be booked and taken in the same window unless an exemption applies or you are resitting one part."}, {"q": "Will you need to memorise tax rates for SQE1?", "a": "No. Where you need a rate or threshold it is given; you need to know when reliefs and exemptions apply."}], "resources": [{"title": "SQE1 Assessment Specification (FLK1 and FLK2)", "url": "https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-specification", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "SQE1 sample questions", "url": "https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe1-assessments/sqe1-sample-questions", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "practice site"}, {"title": "How much the SQE costs (fees and resits)", "url": "https://sqe.sra.org.uk/about/cost", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "SQE assessment dates and booking windows", "url": "https://sqe.sra.org.uk/booking/assessment-dates-locations/booking-windows", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "SQE reports (pass rates and examiner feedback)", "url": "https://sqe.sra.org.uk/sqe-results/reports", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}]}, {"name": "SQE2: practical legal skills", "why": "SQE2 tests whether you can do what a newly qualified solicitor does with a real client file, not just know the law.", "lesson": "SQE2 has 16 assessments: four oral assessments over two half-days, and twelve written assessments over three half-days. The skills tested are client interviewing with an attendance note, advocacy, case and matter analysis, legal research, legal writing and legal drafting, with negotiation built into at least one task. They are set in five practice areas: criminal litigation (including advising at the police station), dispute resolution, property practice, wills and the administration of estates, and business law and practice. Since 1 September 2026, ethics and professional conduct has its own marking criterion, so spotting and handling ethical issues is marked separately. From September 2026 SQE2 costs £3,086. Your training contract seats are your best preparation: use real files (anonymised) to practise.", "subtopics": ["Client interview and attendance note", "Advocacy", "Case and matter analysis", "Legal research and writing", "Legal drafting", "Negotiation", "Ethics as a marked criterion"], "drill": ["Do a 25-minute mock client interview with a colleague, then write the attendance note within 25 minutes.", "Draft a short clause, letter or court document from a sample question and mark it against the SQE2 criteria.", "In each seat, write down one ethical issue you saw and how the firm handled it."], "ready_when": "You can complete each SQE2 sample task within the time limit and your work hits every assessment criterion, including the ethics one.", "pitfalls": ["Giving detailed legal advice in the interview instead of building trust and getting the facts.", "Missing the ethical issue planted in a task.", "Writing long, unfocused advice instead of clear next steps."], "check": [{"q": "How many oral and written assessments are in SQE2?", "a": "Four oral assessments and twelve written ones."}], "resources": [{"title": "SQE2 Assessment Specification", "url": "https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe2-assessments/sqe2-specification", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "SQE2 sample questions", "url": "https://sqe.sra.org.uk/assessments/sqe2-assessments/sqe2-sample-questions", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "practice site"}, {"title": "Statement of Solicitor Competence", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/resources/continuing-competence/competence-statement/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}]}, {"name": "Being an excellent trainee", "why": "Your seats decide where you qualify and whether you are kept on. Partners remember trainees who are reliable, careful and easy to work with.", "lesson": "As a trainee you will run document reviews, draft first versions of letters and agreements, research points of law, manage deal checklists and court bundles, and attend client meetings. The basics matter most: confirm the task, the deadline and the format; keep a list of everything you've been given; flag problems early; check your work twice; and never guess on a point of law or a deadline. Record your work in a QWE log as you go, noting which competences from the Statement of Solicitor Competence each task shows. Ask for feedback at mid-seat and end-of-seat reviews and act on it.", "subtopics": ["Taking instructions: task, deadline, format, budget", "Document management, checklists and bundles", "Research notes and first drafts", "Time recording and billing basics", "Mid-seat and end-of-seat reviews", "QWE log against the Statement of Solicitor Competence"], "drill": ["Keep a daily task list with deadline, supervisor and status, and review it every morning and evening.", "After each piece of work, note one thing the supervisor changed and why.", "Update your QWE log weekly with tasks mapped to competences."], "ready_when": "Supervisors trust you with first drafts that need few changes, you never miss a deadline, and your QWE log is complete and ready to confirm.", "pitfalls": ["Saying yes to everything and then missing deadlines.", "Waiting until the end of two years to record QWE.", "Not telling a supervisor about a mistake straight away."], "check": [{"q": "You realise you sent a draft to the client with an error. What do you do first?", "a": "Tell your supervisor straight away, explain what happened, and help put it right. Honesty and acting in the client's interests come first."}], "resources": [{"title": "Statement of Solicitor Competence", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/resources/continuing-competence/competence-statement/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Qualifying work experience (QWE) rules", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/sqe/qualifying-work-experience-candidates/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Junior Lawyers Division", "url": "https://www.lawsociety.org.uk/topics/junior-lawyers", "provider": "The Law Society", "kind": "reference"}]}, {"name": "Professional conduct and qualifying", "why": "Ethics is tested in both SQE stages and is the basis of your career. Admission also needs character and suitability clearance.", "lesson": "Solicitors are bound by the SRA Principles (including acting with integrity, honesty and independence, and in each client's best interests) and the SRA Code of Conduct (confidentiality, conflicts of interest, client money, undertakings, not misleading the court). When duties conflict, upholding the rule of law and the proper administration of justice comes first. To be admitted you must meet the SRA's character and suitability requirements; declare anything relevant (such as criminal matters or academic misconduct) early and honestly, because concealment is treated far more seriously than most disclosures. Once your QWE is confirmed and you've passed SQE1 and SQE2, you apply for admission to the roll; to practise as a solicitor you then need a practising certificate.", "subtopics": ["SRA Principles", "SRA Code of Conduct: confidentiality, conflicts, undertakings, client money", "Duties to the court", "Character and suitability disclosures", "Admission to the roll and practising certificates"], "drill": ["Read the SRA Principles and write one real-world example of each.", "Work through three ethics scenarios (a conflict, a confidentiality breach, a client asking you to mislead) and write what the Code requires.", "Check whether you have anything to disclose for character and suitability and, if so, read the SRA guidance on disclosing it."], "ready_when": "You can name the Principle or Code paragraph behind any common ethical dilemma, and your admission paperwork is ready.", "pitfalls": ["Treating ethics as common sense rather than learning the actual rules.", "Not disclosing a character issue, which is worse than the issue itself."], "check": [{"q": "Two Principles conflict. Which generally wins?", "a": "Those that safeguard the wider public interest, such as the rule of law and public confidence in the profession, take precedence over an individual client's interests."}], "resources": [{"title": "SRA Principles", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/standards-regulations/principles/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/standards-regulations/code-conduct-solicitors/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Character and suitability", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/character-and-suitability/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Admission to the roll: forms and fees", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/become-solicitor/admission/admission-forms-fees/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}]}], "project": {"title": "Qualification file", "brief": "Keep one file that proves you're ready to qualify: SQE results, a complete QWE record, and evidence of your skills.", "steps": ["Create a SQE1 study plan from the specification, with weekly practice targets and an error log.", "Keep a QWE log from day one: dates, organisation, supervisor, tasks, and competences shown.", "Collect anonymised examples of your work for each SQE2 skill (an attendance note, a piece of drafting, a research note, a letter).", "Before each seat change, ask your supervisor for written feedback and record what you'll improve.", "Line up the solicitor or COLP who will confirm your QWE and check what evidence they want.", "Complete the character and suitability and admission steps on the SRA site."], "done_when": "You have passed SQE1 and SQE2, your QWE has been confirmed, and your admission application is submitted."}, "evidence": ["SQE1 Assessment Specification (applies from 1 September 2026): FLK1 and FLK2 subjects, single-best-answer questions with five options, closed book, ethics tested throughout, law cut-off four months before the window.", "SQE2 Assessment Specification (from 1 September 2026): four oral assessments over two half-days and twelve written assessments over three half-days; six skills in five practice areas; separate ethics and professional conduct criterion.", "SQE fees from September 2026: SQE1 £2,006 (£1,003 per FLK), SQE2 £3,086 (sqe.sra.org.uk/about/cost)."]}, {"level": "Expert", "goal": "Qualify into a team, build expertise as an associate, and move towards senior associate, legal director or partner. Develop deep knowledge of one area, manage matters and people, win the trust of clients, and keep up your continuing competence.", "weeks": 260, "plan": [{"weeks": "1-52", "focus": "Newly qualified (NQ): own your first matters under supervision, learn the team's precedents and processes, and build relationships with clients' junior lawyers."}, {"weeks": "53-156", "focus": "Associate (1 to 3 years' post-qualification experience): run matters day to day, supervise trainees, and start writing and speaking on your area."}, {"weeks": "157-260", "focus": "Senior associate track: lead deals or cases, contribute to pitches, manage budgets, and build a reputation clients ask for by name. Decide between partnership, in-house, or a specialist role."}], "skills": [{"name": "Deep expertise in a practice area", "why": "Clients pay for judgment in a specific area. Specialism is what moves you from a capable associate to someone clients and partners rely on.", "lesson": "Pick an area where you enjoy the work and there is lasting client demand. Learn its statutes, leading cases and market practice, and the commercial context your clients work in. Build your own precedents and checklists. Keep current through legal updates and the courts' and regulators' websites, and record your learning, because the SRA requires you to reflect on and maintain your competence every year.", "subtopics": ["Leading statutes and cases in your area", "Market practice and standard documents", "Client sectors and their commercial pressures", "Continuing competence records"], "drill": ["Build a one-page map of the key law, regulators and documents in your area.", "Write a short update for clients on one change in the law, and get a partner to review it.", "Each quarter, record what you learned and what you'll develop next."], "ready_when": "Colleagues come to you with questions in your area, and you can give a client a clear answer and the risks on a routine point the same day.", "pitfalls": ["Specialising too early, before seeing what you enjoy.", "Letting knowledge drift out of date."], "check": [{"q": "Do solicitors have to log a set number of CPD hours?", "a": "No. The SRA's continuing competence approach asks you to reflect on your practice, identify learning needs, and plan, carry out and record your development."}], "resources": [{"title": "Continuing competence for solicitors", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/resources/topic/continuing-competence/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Find Case Law: judgments of the courts of England and Wales", "url": "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/", "provider": "The National Archives", "kind": "Free primary text"}, {"title": "legislation.gov.uk: UK statutes, as amended", "url": "https://www.legislation.gov.uk/", "provider": "The National Archives", "kind": "Free primary text"}]}, {"name": "Running matters and supervising others", "why": "From about two years after qualifying, you're judged on how well matters run: on time, on budget and without surprises for the client.", "lesson": "Running a matter means scoping the work, agreeing the budget, setting a timeline, delegating tasks to trainees and juniors with clear instructions, checking their work, and keeping the client informed. Good supervisors explain the purpose of a task, the deadline and the format, give feedback quickly, and take responsibility for what goes out. Keep a matter plan and update it as things change.", "subtopics": ["Scoping and budgeting", "Matter plans and checklists", "Delegation and review", "Client updates and managing expectations", "Giving feedback"], "drill": ["Write a matter plan for a current matter: steps, owners, dates, budget and risks.", "After delegating a task, ask the trainee what was unclear and improve your next instruction."], "ready_when": "Your matters finish on budget with no last-minute surprises, and trainees ask to work with you.", "pitfalls": ["Doing everything yourself instead of delegating.", "Only telling the client about a budget overrun after it has happened."], "check": [{"q": "What four things should every delegated task include?", "a": "What the task is for, the deadline, the format you want, and who to ask if stuck."}], "resources": [{"title": "SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/standards-regulations/code-conduct-solicitors/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}, {"title": "Statement of Solicitor Competence", "url": "https://www.sra.org.uk/solicitors/resources/continuing-competence/competence-statement/", "provider": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "kind": "official"}]}, {"name": "Clients, business development and career choices", "why": "Seniority depends on clients trusting you and wanting to work with you, and on choosing the right next step for you.", "lesson": "Business development starts early: keep in touch with the junior lawyers you work with at clients, because they become the general counsel who instruct firms later. Write and speak on your area. Help on pitches. At senior associate level, decide whether partnership suits you (it is a business ownership role, judged on client relationships and revenue as well as legal skill) or whether in-house, a legal director or counsel role, or a move abroad fits better.", "subtopics": ["Building relationships with clients' lawyers", "Writing and speaking", "Pitches and tenders", "Partnership, in-house and other paths"], "drill": ["List 15 people at clients and other firms you've worked with and plan one useful contact with each this year.", "Write and publish one practical article in your area."], "ready_when": "Clients ask for you by name and you have a clear view of which senior path you want.", "pitfalls": ["Waiting until you're senior to build relationships.", "Assuming partnership is the only definition of success."], "check": [{"q": "Why do clients' junior lawyers matter to your career?", "a": "They become the decision-makers who choose which firms and lawyers to instruct."}], "resources": [{"title": "The Lawyer: UK legal market news", "url": "https://www.thelawyer.com/", "provider": "The Lawyer", "kind": "reference"}, {"title": "Legal Futures: legal services and regulation news", "url": "https://www.legalfutures.co.uk/", "provider": "Legal Futures", "kind": "reference"}]}], "project": {"title": "Specialist portfolio", "brief": "Show you are a trusted specialist: real (anonymised) work products, published thinking and a development record.", "steps": ["Write a one-page map of your practice area's key law, regulators and standard documents.", "Prepare a matter plan and budget for a matter you are running, and a short review after its next key stage.", "Publish one practical article or client update, and give one internal training session for trainees.", "Keep a continuing competence record for the year: what you identified, planned, did and learned."], "done_when": "A partner or senior colleague would use your portfolio to support your promotion case."}, "evidence": ["SRA continuing competence: solicitors reflect on their practice, identify learning needs, and plan, address and record their development, instead of logging fixed CPD hours.", "SRA Statement of Solicitor Competence sets out the competences expected of solicitors, used for QWE and for the SQE."]}], "tools": [{"name": "Chambers Student and Legal Cheek deadline calendars", "use": "Track training contract, vacation scheme and open day deadlines across firms.", "free_option": "Free at chambers.com/student and legalcheek.com; confirm every date on the firm's own careers page."}, {"name": "LawCareers.Net and targetjobs", "use": "Firm profiles (places, requirements, deadlines) and live training contract listings.", "free_option": "Free at lawcareers.net and targetjobs.co.uk."}, {"name": "legislation.gov.uk", "use": "Read the current text of statutes and regulations, with amendments.", "free_option": "Free, from The National Archives."}, {"name": "Find Case Law and BAILII", "use": "Read judgments from the courts of England and Wales.", "free_option": "Free at caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk and bailii.org."}, {"name": "SQE sample questions", "use": "Official examples of SQE1 single-best-answer questions and SQE2 tasks.", "free_option": "Free at sqe.sra.org.uk."}, {"name": "Critical thinking test practice", "use": "Practise the Watson Glaser format many firms use to screen applicants.", "free_option": "Free practice tests at watsonglaser.co.uk; many university careers services offer more."}, {"name": "Forage job simulations", "use": "Do realistic tasks set by law firms to test your interest and gain material for applications.", "free_option": "Free at theforage.com."}, {"name": "Microsoft Word and Excel", "use": "Drafting with tracked changes and comparison, deal checklists, trackers for applications and QWE.", "free_option": "Word and Excel on the web are free with a Microsoft account; LibreOffice is a free alternative."}, {"name": "A QWE log", "use": "Record dates, supervisors, tasks and competences shown, so your QWE can be confirmed easily.", "free_option": "Any spreadsheet; map each task to the SRA Statement of Solicitor Competence."}], "ladder": [{"title": "Student, paralegal or vacation scheme student", "years": "Before qualifying (often 1 to 3 years)", "moves_you_up": "Specific applications, strong test scores, real legal experience, and a good vacation scheme."}, {"title": "Trainee solicitor (training contract)", "years": "2 years, usually four six-month seats", "moves_you_up": "Reliable, careful work, good seat reviews, passing the SQE, and a team that wants to keep you."}, {"title": "Newly qualified (NQ) solicitor", "years": "0 to 1 years post-qualification experience (PQE)", "moves_you_up": "Owning matters, accurate drafting, good client communication."}, {"title": "Associate", "years": "1 to 4 years PQE", "moves_you_up": "Running matters day to day, supervising trainees, building specialist knowledge."}, {"title": "Senior associate", "years": "Around 5 to 8 years PQE", "moves_you_up": "Leading matters, client relationships, business development, managing budgets and teams."}, {"title": "Legal director, counsel or partner", "years": "Usually 8+ years PQE", "moves_you_up": "A client following, revenue, leadership and a market reputation in your area."}], "credentials": [{"name": "Degree or equivalent qualification or experience (any subject)", "issuer": "University or equivalent, as accepted by the SRA", "when": "Required before admission on the SQE route; apprentices meet it through the apprenticeship."}, {"name": "SQE1 (FLK1 and FLK2)", "issuer": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "when": "Required. Usually taken before or early in a training contract; £2,006 from September 2026."}, {"name": "SQE2", "issuer": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "when": "Required. Can only be taken after passing SQE1; often sat during QWE. £3,086 from September 2026."}, {"name": "Two years of qualifying work experience (QWE)", "issuer": "Confirmed by a solicitor or COLP", "when": "Required. Up to four organisations; a training contract is the most common way."}, {"name": "Character and suitability clearance", "issuer": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "when": "Required before admission."}, {"name": "Admission to the roll of solicitors", "issuer": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "when": "After all requirements are met."}, {"name": "Practising certificate", "issuer": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "when": "Required to practise as a solicitor, renewed each year."}, {"name": "Solicitor apprenticeship (level 7)", "issuer": "Employer and training provider", "when": "Optional alternative route: earn while qualifying, no degree needed."}, {"name": "Legal Practice Course with a period of recognised training", "issuer": "LPC provider and the SRA", "when": "Only for people who started a qualifying law degree, GDL or LPC before September 2021; the route closes at the end of 2032."}, {"name": "Higher Rights of Audience", "issuer": "Solicitors Regulation Authority", "when": "Optional after qualifying, for solicitors who want to appear as advocates in the higher courts."}], "interview": [{"question": "Why do you want to be a solicitor rather than a barrister?", "strong_answer": "Point to the work itself: long-term client relationships, teamwork on transactions or cases, and being involved from start to finish. Back it up with something you've seen, such as a vacation scheme task or clinic work."}, {"question": "Why this firm?", "strong_answer": "Give two or three specifics: a practice area or sector strength, a recent matter, the seat structure or secondments, and link each to your own experience. Avoid prestige and generic training claims."}, {"question": "Tell me about a commercial story you've followed recently.", "strong_answer": "Explain the story in two sentences, then the commercial drivers, the risks, and which lawyers would be instructed and what they'd do. Finish with your own view."}, {"question": "Tell me about a time you made a mistake.", "strong_answer": "Pick a real but recoverable mistake, show that you raised it quickly and honestly, explain how you fixed it, and say what you now do differently."}, {"question": "How would you prioritise three urgent tasks from three different partners?", "strong_answer": "Confirm each deadline and how important it is, tell the partners about the clash early, agree priorities rather than guessing, and keep everyone updated."}, {"question": "Walk me through how a company might fund an acquisition.", "strong_answer": "Cash reserves, new debt (bank loans or bonds) or issuing shares, and the trade-offs: debt is cheaper but adds risk; shares dilute owners. Mention which lawyers act on each part."}, {"question": "What does the SQE test and how will you prepare?", "strong_answer": "SQE1 tests legal knowledge across FLK1 and FLK2 with single-best-answer questions; SQE2 tests practical skills in five practice areas. Describe a structured plan with timed practice and an error log."}, {"question": "A client asks you to keep information from the other side that you're legally required to disclose. What do you do?", "strong_answer": "Explain you can't mislead the court or the other side, that your duty to the administration of justice comes first, and that you'd tell your supervisor and advise the client of the consequences."}, {"question": "What would you do in your first week of a seat?", "strong_answer": "Learn the team's matters and processes, confirm how your supervisor likes work presented, set up a task list, and ask for a mid-seat review date."}, {"question": "Where do you see yourself in five years?", "strong_answer": "Qualified into a team you've enjoyed, running matters, supervising trainees and building a specialism, ideally at this firm. Keep it grounded in the work."}], "glossary": [{"term": "SRA", "meaning": "Solicitors Regulation Authority: the regulator that sets qualification rules and the SQE."}, {"term": "SQE", "meaning": "Solicitors Qualifying Examination, in two stages: SQE1 (legal knowledge) and SQE2 (practical legal skills)."}, {"term": "FLK", "meaning": "Functioning Legal Knowledge: the two SQE1 exams, FLK1 and FLK2."}, {"term": "QWE", "meaning": "Qualifying work experience: two years' full-time or equivalent legal work, in up to four organisations, confirmed by a solicitor or COLP."}, {"term": "Training contract", "meaning": "A two-year trainee role at a firm, usually across four seats; the most structured way to complete QWE."}, {"term": "Seat", "meaning": "A rotation (usually six months) in one practice area during a training contract."}, {"term": "Vacation scheme", "meaning": "A paid work placement of one to four weeks, often the main route into a firm's training contract."}, {"term": "Insight scheme / open day", "meaning": "Short introductory events, often for first-year students, that can lead to fast-tracked vacation scheme applications."}, {"term": "NQ", "meaning": "Newly qualified solicitor."}, {"term": "PQE", "meaning": "Post-qualification experience: years since qualifying, used to describe seniority."}, {"term": "Retention rate", "meaning": "The share of a firm's qualifying trainees kept on as NQs."}, {"term": "COLP", "meaning": "Compliance Officer for Legal Practice: the person responsible for a firm's regulatory compliance; can confirm QWE."}, {"term": "Magic Circle", "meaning": "An informal name for a group of the largest London-headquartered law firms."}, {"term": "Watson Glaser", "meaning": "A critical thinking test used by many firms to screen applicants."}, {"term": "LPC / PGDL", "meaning": "Legal Practice Course (the older vocational stage) and Postgraduate Diploma in Law (a law conversion course)."}, {"term": "Character and suitability", "meaning": "The SRA's check that you are fit to be admitted, covering matters such as criminal records and academic misconduct."}, {"term": "Admission to the roll", "meaning": "Formally becoming a solicitor once all requirements are met."}, {"term": "Practising certificate", "meaning": "The annual SRA certificate you need to practise as a solicitor."}, {"term": "Higher Rights of Audience", "meaning": "An extra qualification letting solicitors appear as advocates in the higher courts."}, {"term": "Graduate visa", "meaning": "A UK visa to stay and work after an eligible UK degree: 2 years if you apply on or before 31 December 2026, 18 months from 1 January 2027."}], "mistakes": ["Applying late. Many firms review applications as they arrive and fill places before the published deadline.", "Sending the same 'why this firm' answer to every firm.", "Assuming listing sites are right: always confirm deadlines on the firm's own careers page.", "Paying for SQE preparation yourself before checking whether a future employer would fund it.", "Not keeping a QWE log from day one, then struggling to get two years confirmed.", "For international candidates: not checking visa sponsorship and the Graduate visa timing before choosing firms.", "Treating the vacation scheme as a holiday rather than a two to four-week interview.", "Ignoring regional, in-house and paralegal routes, which can lead to qualification too."], "checked_at": "2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z"}$rm$::jsonb, now())
